also this morning voters in iran are  electing a new president  who will still be under the control of  religious leaders the election comes in  the middle of talks on reviving the  nuclear deal with the u.s and other  countries  and voters there are still more  concerned about the country's severe  economic  troubles elizabeth palmer is in iran's  capital this morning for us liz good  morning to you  good morning in conservative areas of  iran with a large religious population  voters have already begun showing up you  can see some of them at this tehran  polling station behind me  but the question is how many of the 60  million  eligible voters overall will actually  bother to cast a ballot  the authorities are worried about an  embarrassingly  low turnout the leading presidential  candidate ibrahim raisi  has no appeal for many middle-class  people  or especially the young he's a senior  judge close to iran's supreme leader ali  khamenei  and a real hardliner if he wins he'd be  the first iranian president to take  office  already personally under sanctioned by  the united states  that's for his part in the crackdown  after protests in 2009  and his alleged supervision of thousands  of executions during iran's revolution  as for the nuclear deal currently being  renegotiated in vienna  ricey said he would respect it if it's  signed  but in the three years since president  trump pulled the us  out of that deal iran has ramped up its  nuclear program  raising would take office with a  stockpile of enriched uranium that's  closer to weapons grade  than at any time in history that was  elizabeth palmer reporting from  iran
 

